+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a value like `90`, `45s`, `1h30m`, or `2d` into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`.
+
+A plain whole number is a number of seconds. Otherwise the value is one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit:
+`d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may be given at most once, in that order,
+without spaces. Units are case-insensitive, so `1H30M` is the same as `1h30m`. Negative numbers, decimals, unknown units,
+and spaces are rejected. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta`, such as a `default`, is passed through unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    import datetime
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default=datetime.timedelta(minutes=5))
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=120'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6.0
+```
+
